 Decision making under uncertainty is at the core operation of intelligent autonomous agents and robots. Autonomous navigation, robotic surgeries and automated warehousing are only a few examples where agents must autonomously plan and execute their actions while reasoning about uncertainty. Such uncertainty might be due to noisy or limited observations; imprecise delivery  of the agent's state; or due to the inherent uncertainty of the environment. In robotic applications, the uncertainty is often modeled by a probability distribution over the state space, known as a belief. The agent then must reason about this belief to find an optimal action sequence, known as Belief Space Planning (BSP) \cite{arxivID-1304.2343-0}. In BSP, the agent must determine which actions will lead to the most informative observations and reduce the uncertainty of the belief. However, in environments with ambiguous and perceptually aliased observations, the agent must also reason about data association, which further increases the complexity of the problem.

Data association is the process of identifying the relationship between the observations and the elements of the environment. In an aliased environment, multiple elements are indistinguishable by the agent and the agent must determine which elements correspond to which observations. This problem is known as the data association problem, and it is a crucial component of many robotic applications, including Simultaneous Localization and Mapping (SLAM) \cite{arxivID-1002.4820-1}, and object recognition \cite{arxivID-cs/0208005-2}.

In BSP, the data association problem is compounded by the fact that the agent must reason about it while planning. The agent must consider all possible data associations and plan accordingly, which leads to an exponential growth in the number of hypotheses with the planning horizon. To mitigate this problem, most approaches in the literature rely on approximations to reduce the computational complexity of the problem \cite{arxivID-1304.2757-3, arxivID-1304.1094-4, arxivID-1304.1109-5, arxivID-2109.11808-6, arxivID-1502.02474-7, arxivID-1712.09716-8, arxivID-1703.04211-9, arxivID-1509.08155-10, arxivID-1605.00335-11, arxivID-2209.11591-12, arxivID-2203.02381-13, arxivID-2209.12427-14, arxivID-2209.12427-15, arxivID-2002.08124-16}. However, these approximations often come at the cost of reduced accuracy and performance guarantees.

In this work, we present an approach to BSP that reasons about data association and provides performance guarantees. Our approach utilizes a subset of hypotheses, known as a distilled subset, to solve the BSP problem. We derive error bounds with respect to the optimal solution and demonstrate that the error is bounded by the size of the distilled subset. We then use the error bounds to determine the number of hypotheses needed to achieve a desired level of accuracy. Our approach is applicable to a wide range of BSP problems and can be used to solve problems with hard budget constraints, where some non-negligible hypotheses must be pruned. We demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach in an extremely aliased environment, where we manage to significantly reduce computation time without compromising on the quality of the solution.

The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section \ref{sec:related_work} provides an overview of the relevant literature on BSP and data association. Section \ref{sec:problem_formulation} presents the problem formulation and Section \ref{sec:methodology} describes our approach. Section \ref{sec:experimental_results} presents the experimental results, and Section \ref{sec:conclusion} concludes the paper.
Related Work
\label{sec:related_work}

\subsection{Belief Space Planning}
BSP is a well-studied problem in the robotics literature, and many approaches have been proposed to solve it. Early works, such as \cite{arxivID-1304.1094-17, arxivID-1304.1109-18}, proposed heuristic-based methods for BSP. These methods are computationally efficient but do not provide performance guarantees. More recently, information-theoretic approaches have been proposed to solve the BSP problem \cite{arxivID-2109.11808-19, arxivID-1502.02474-20, arxivID-1712.09716-21, arxivID-1703.04211-22, arxivID-1509.08155-23}

