In robotic applications, such as autonomous driving or search and rescue, it is crucial to reason about the robots' own state uncertainty. To this end, Belief Space Planning (BSP) has been shown to be an effective approach to solve such problems. BSP is a sequential decision-making framework where the robot plans over a set of possible states, called the belief space. The belief space is obtained by integrating all possible observations with the robot's motion model. However, when the environment is ambiguous, BSP will also reason about all possible observations and environment states. This leads to an exponential growth of the belief space with the planning horizon, which quickly becomes intractable. To avoid such an exponential growth, previous work on BSP has considered only a subset of the possible observations and environment states. For instance, in the context of autonomous driving, the environment is considered as a static map and the robot is assumed to have perfect knowledge of its own state \cite{arxivID-2108.07993-0}. In the context of search and rescue, the robot is assumed to have perfect knowledge of its own state, while the environment state is modeled as a set of discrete points, whose positions are estimated by the robot \cite{arxivID-2107.08834-1}. In general, however, it is difficult to make such assumptions, which can lead to sub-optimal solutions or even catastrophic results.

To overcome the limitations of previous work, in this paper we present a novel approach to BSP that explicitly reasons about all possible data associations. We achieve this by distilling the belief space to a subset of hypotheses, where each hypothesis is associated with a specific data association. By reasoning about data association, we can significantly reduce the number of hypotheses, which in turn reduces the computational complexity of the problem. We also provide performance guarantees by deriving error bounds with respect to the optimal solution. To demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach, we conduct experiments in an extremely aliased environment, where we manage to significantly reduce computation time without compromising on the quality of the solution.

Our contributions can be summarized as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item We present a novel approach to BSP that explicitly reasons about data association, which leads to significant reductions in computation time.
\item We derive error bounds with respect to the optimal solution, which provides performance guarantees.
\item We demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach in an extremely aliased environment, where we manage to significantly reduce computation time without compromising on the quality of the solution.
\end{itemize} <|section_end|>

