 The goal of speech enhancement (SE) is to convert low-quality speech signals to ones with improved quality and intelligibility. SE serves as an important regression task in the speech-processing field and has been widely used for a pre-processor in speech-related applications, such as speech coding #ref1#, automatic speech recognition (ASR) #ref2#, speaker recognition #ref3#, and assistive hearing devices #ref4# #ref5#. Recent advances in machine learning have made  significant progress in SE, especially in the case of deep neural networks (DNNs) and their variants. However, when the noisy speech is in a domain different from the training data, the performance of the trained model degrades. Therefore, domain adaptation (DA) is required to adapt the model to the target domain.

\subsection{Related Work}
The DA problem has been studied in various fields, such as computer vision (CV) and natural language processing (NLP). In particular, many domain adaptation methods have been proposed for classification tasks, especially for image classification, including domain adversarial training \cite{arxivID-1412.4446-0}, and feature alignment \cite{arxivID-1511.05547-1, arxivID-1711.06114-2}. However, in contrast to the abundance of literature on DA for classification, the literature on DA for regression is much more scarce. The main challenge for DA in regression is that regression models are inherently more sensitive to distributional shifts, and the label space in regression is continuous.

Some domain adaptation methods have been proposed for speech-related applications. For example, in speech enhancement, the domain gap between the training and testing data is caused by the mismatch between the noise types. To address this issue, \cite{arxivID-1807.07501-3} used domain adversarial training (DAT) to tackle the mismatch between stationary and non-stationary noises. In speech recognition, \cite{arxivID-2109.05494-4} used DAT and domain separation networks to adapt to low-resource languages. For speaker recognition, \cite{arxivID-2011.08623-5} used adversarial training to solve the multi-domain mismatch problem.

\subsection{Contributions}
In this paper, we propose a novel discriminator-constrained optimal transport network (DOTN) to perform unsupervised DA for SE. Our proposed DOTN approach tactically fuses the optimal transport (OT) theory from mathematical analysis with generative adversarial frameworks, to help evaluate continuous labels in the target domain. Specifically, we extend the classical OT formulation by adding a discriminator to constrain the optimal coupling, which aims to minimize the cost between the source and target domains while keeping the conditional distribution of the target domain close to the source domain. The proposed DOTN is trained in a purely unsupervised manner, which does not require any clean speech in the target domain. We compare our DOTN approach with previous adversarial domain adaptation frameworks in two SE tasks, and the experimental results show that our DOTN outperforms previous methods in a purely unsupervised manner. <|section_end|>

