The domain shift can be addressed by exploiting the knowledge available from the source domain, while adapting the model to the target domain. Various domain adaptation (DA) methods have been developed in recent years, which can be broadly categorized into two groups: supervised and unsupervised DA. Supervised DA methods require additional information, such as labels in the target domain, to adapt the model. However, such information is often not available in real-world applications. In contrast, unsupervised DA methods, which do not require such information, are more practical and have been extensively studied in recent years. For example, several approaches have been proposed to leverage the domain-invariant features using deep neural networks (DNNs) #ref6# #ref7# #ref8# or to align the distributions between the source and target domains using adversarial learning #ref9# #ref10#.

In this study, we propose a novel DA approach for SE based on the optimal transport (OT) theory. The proposed approach is based on the classical OT formulation, which aims to find the optimal transport plan between two probability distributions. The OT formulation can be used to measure the discrepancy between two distributions, which can be leveraged to align the distributions between the source and target domains. In particular, we propose to use the OT formulation to estimate the mapping function between noisy and clean speech signals in the target domain, by exploiting the knowledge available from the source domain. To achieve this, we propose to use a generative adversarial network (GAN) framework, which has been shown to be effective in many DA tasks. The proposed approach is purely unsupervised, which means that it does not require any additional information about the target domain, such as labels or domain information.

