 Although deep neural networks have achieved great success in a variety of Natural Language Processing (NLP) tasks, recent studies show their vulnerability to malicious perturbations  #ref12# #ref16# #ref11# #ref18#. By adding imperceptible perturbations (e.g. typos or synonym substitutions) to original input text, attackers  can easily fool deep neural networks to make incorrect predictions. This raises serious concerns about the security of deep neural networks and impedes the deployment of deep learning models in real-world applications. Therefore, how to improve the adversarial robustness of deep learning models is an important challenge.

Previous studies reveal that the adversarial vulnerability of deep neural networks is caused by the non-robust features in supervised training. These non-robust features are easily changed by small perturbations, which leads to incorrect predictions \cite{arxivID-1905.02175-0}. In NLP tasks, the non-robust features are often caused by the spurious correlations between the noise and the label. For example, in the text classification task, the model may learn that the label is positively correlated with a certain word. So when this word is replaced by its synonym, the model can not make correct predictions. Thus, it is necessary to discard these non-robust features to improve the adversarial robustness of the model.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.48\textwidth]{figures/figure1.pdf}
\caption{The illustration of the disentangled text representation learning. The left part is the robust feature, and the right part is the non-robust feature. The text representations are disentangled by the robust and non-robust features.}
\label{fig:1}
\end{figure}

Recently, some researches have proposed to improve the adversarial robustness of NLP models from the view of disentangled representation learning. They assume that the text representation is a mixture of robust and non-robust features. To defend against the adversarial attacks, they try to disentangle the robust and non-robust features. Then they use the robust features to make predictions. However, these methods do not consider the semantic representativeness of the robust features. In other words, the robust features may lose the semantic information of the input text, which leads to incorrect predictions. Therefore, how to learn a robust feature with both semantic representativeness and adversarial robustness is an important challenge.

In this paper, we tackle the adversarial robustness challenge from the view of disentangled representation learning. We assume that the text representation is a mixture of robust and non-robust features. As shown in Figure \ref{fig:1}, the robust feature is a semantic representation that is robust to small perturbations. The non-robust feature is a non-semantic representation that is easily changed by small perturbations. The text representation is disentangled by the robust and non-robust features. Thus, we need to learn a robust feature with both semantic representativeness and adversarial robustness. To this end, we propose a disentangled learning method with the information-theoretic perspective. Inspired by the variation of information (VI) in information theory \cite{arxivID-1006.0375-1}, we derive a disentangled learning objective composed of mutual information to represent both the semantic representativeness of latent embeddings and differentiation of robust and non-robust features. On the basis of this, we design a disentangled learning network to estimate these mutual information. Experiments on text classification and entailment tasks show that our method significantly outperforms the representative methods under adversarial attacks, indicating that discarding non-robust features is critical for improving adversarial robustness. The main contributions of this paper are summarized as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item We propose a disentangled learning method with the information-theoretic perspective to improve the adversarial robustness of NLP models. To the best of our knowledge, we are the first to tackle the adversarial robustness challenge from the view of disentangled representation learning with the information-theoretic perspective.
\item We propose a novel disentangled learning objective composed of mutual information to represent both the semantic representativeness of latent embeddings and differentiation of robust and non-robust features.
\item We conduct experiments on text classification and entailment tasks to evaluate the effectiveness of our method. The results demonstrate that our method significantly outperforms the representative methods under adversarial attacks.
\end{itemize}
