\subsection{Adversarial Attacks in NLP}
In recent years, the adversarial attacks in NLP have attracted much attention \cite{arxivID-1901.06796-2}. The goal of adversarial attacks is to fool the deep neural networks to make incorrect predictions. By adding imperceptible perturbations to the original input text, attackers can easily fool the model to make incorrect predictions. In general, the adversarial attacks in NLP can be divided into three categories: 1) \textbf{Character-level attacks}: the attackers modify the characters of the original input text, such as typos or synonym substitutions. 2) \textbf{Word-level attacks}: the attackers modify the words of the original input text, such as deleting, inserting or replacing words. 3) \textbf{Sentence-level attacks}: the attackers modify the sentence structure of the original input text, such as word reordering. 

