 The fusion of deep learning with two-player games has produced a wealth of breakthroughs in recent years, from Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) #ref9# through to model-based reinforcement learning #ref37# #ref29#. Gradient descent methods are widely used across these settings, partly because these algorithms scale well to high-dimensional models and large datasets. However, much of the recent progress in our theoretical understanding of two-player differentiable games builds upon the analysis of continuous -time gradient dynamics. The discrepancy between continuous and discrete dynamics can be particularly pronounced in two-player games, and this can lead to a range of challenges including oscillation, instability, and convergence to poor local minima.

In this paper, we take a first step towards resolving the discrepancy between continuous and discrete dynamics by analysing the discretization drift of gradient-based algorithms in two-player games. We focus on the simple and widely used class of simultaneous and alternating gradient descent methods. The main insight of our work is that discretization drift is not an artifact of the numerical integration scheme, but rather a feature of the underlying continuous dynamics. We derive modified continuous-time dynamical systems that closely follow the discrete dynamics of simultaneous and alternating gradient descent. These modified dynamics provide an insight into the challenges associated with two-player games, including GANs. In particular, we identify distinct components of the discretization drift that can alter performance and in some cases destabilize the game. Finally, quantifying discretization drift allows us to identify regularizers that explicitly cancel harmful forms of drift or strengthen beneficial forms of drift, and thus improve performance of GAN training.

