Related Work
\paragraph{Simultaneous and alternating gradient descent.}
In simultaneous gradient descent, the players update their strategies in parallel at each iteration. For a zero-sum game, this is equivalent to the gradient flow dynamics, and is known to converge to the Nash equilibrium under certain conditions \cite{arxivID-1804.05464-0}. In contrast, alternating gradient descent alternates between updating the two players in turn. This is equivalent to a discretization of the continuous gradient flow dynamics. In two-player zero-sum games, alternating gradient descent converges to the Nash equilibrium under certain conditions \cite{arxivID-1907.04392-1, arxivID-1907.04392-2}. In this paper, we study the difference between simultaneous and alternating gradient descent, and how this difference arises from the discretization of the continuous gradient flow dynamics. 

\paragraph{Backward error analysis.} Backward error analysis is a powerful tool for understanding the behaviour of numerical integrators for ordinary differential equations. In particular, the method of modified equations provides a systematic approach to understanding the long-term behaviour of numerical integrators \cite{arxivID-2201.03911-3}. In this paper, we use backward error analysis to understand the difference between the continuous and discrete dynamics in two-player games. We derive modified continuous dynamics that closely follow the discrete dynamics of simultaneous and alternating gradient descent. <|section_end|>

