 Binary classification models are used for online decision-making in a wide variety of practical settings. These settings include bank lending  #ref45# #ref23# #ref43#, criminal recidivism prediction  #ref44# #ref47# #ref5#, credit card fraud  #ref7# #ref42#, spam detection  #ref21# #ref40#, self-driving motion planning  #ref36# #ref29#, and recommendation systems  #ref37# #ref11# #ref19#. In many of these tasks, the model only receives  feedback on the true label when a positive decision is made. For example, in a lending task, the model only observes whether a customer repaid a loan if the loan is issued to begin with. If the model mistakenly decides not to issue a loan to a customer who would have repaid it, then the model never observes whether the customer would have repaid the loan. Thus, the model never has the opportunity to correct its mistake. This problem is known as the \emph{class imbalance problem} or \emph{label shift problem} \cite{arxivID-1106.4557-0, arxivID-2309.01357-1, arxivID-1802.03916-2, arxivID-1206.4677-3, arxivID-2206.02449-4, arxivID-2003.07554-5, arxivID-1903.09734-6, arxivID-2312.07282-7, arxivID-2211.04274-8}. In the extreme case, the model may get stuck with a model that never corrects its false negatives, resulting in infinite regret.

In this paper, we study this problem in the context of a bank loan problem. We assume that the bank has access to a model $\hat{f}$ that predicts whether a customer will repay a loan if they are issued one. The bank's goal is to use this model to make decisions that minimize the number of bad loans. However, the bank only observes whether a customer repaid a loan if the loan is issued to begin with. Thus, the bank must make decisions based on a biased dataset that only includes the true labels of customers who repaid their loans.

We propose a method for addressing this problem in the case of linear models. Our method, which we call \emph{Pseudo-Label Optimism} (\texttt{PLOT}), adds optimism directly into the model predictions. \texttt{PLOT} works by adding an optimistic label to the subset of decision points the current model is deciding on, training the model on all data so far (including these points along with their optimistic labels), and finally using the resulting optimistic model for decision making. We show that \texttt{PLOT} satisfies a logarithmic regret guarantee, under a Lipschitz and logistic mean label model, and under a separability condition on the data.

We also extend \texttt{PLOT} to the function approximation case using Deep Neural Networks. We call this method \emph{Deep Pseudo-Label Optimism} (\texttt{Deep PLOT}). \texttt{Deep PLOT} is conceptually and computationally simple, and achieves competitive performance on a set of three challenging benchmark problems, requiring minimal hyperparameter tuning.

