Related Work
\subsection{Adversarial attacks}

Adversarial attacks are a type of attack where an adversary intentionally adds small perturbations to the input data in order to fool the model into making incorrect predictions. These perturbations are often imperceptible to humans but can have a significant impact on the model's performance. Adversarial attacks can be used to evaluate the robustness of models and to improve their robustness by making them more resistant to such attacks.

\subsection{Model compression}

Model compression techniques are used to reduce the size and complexity of neural networks without significantly compromising their performance. These techniques include pruning, quantization, knowledge distillation, etc. Pruning involves removing redundant or unimportant parameters from the model, while quantization reduces the precision of the model's weights and activations. Knowledge distillation involves training a smaller model to mimic the behavior of a larger model, which can be used to reduce the size of the model without significantly impacting its performance. Model compression techniques are important for deploying models on edge devices, as they can reduce the memory and computational requirements of the model.

\subsection{Adversarial attacks on compressed models}

Adversarial attacks on compressed models have been shown to be more effective than attacks on uncompressed models. This is because the compression process can make the model more vulnerable to adversarial attacks by reducing its robustness and making it easier for an attacker to find adversarial examples. Adversarial attacks on compressed models can have serious consequences, particularly in safety-critical applications such as autonomous driving and medical diagnosis. Therefore, it is important to study the robustness of compressed models against adversarial attacks and to develop techniques to improve their robustness.

\subsection{Adversarial attacks on Vision Transformers}

Vision Transformers (ViTs) have been shown to be vulnerable to adversarial attacks, particularly in image classification tasks. Adversarial attacks on ViTs can be divided into two categories: targeted attacks and untargeted attacks. Targeted attacks aim to cause the model to misclassify the input image as a specific target class, while untargeted attacks aim to cause the model to make any incorrect prediction. Adversarial attacks on ViTs can be more effective than attacks on convolutional neural networks (CNNs) due to their ability to capture long-range dependencies and their larger receptive field. However, ViTs are also more computationally expensive and require more memory than CNNs, which can make them less suitable for deployment on edge devices.

