\subsection{Context-aware Feature Fusion}
Context-aware feature fusion is a technique that aims to combine features from different levels of a neural network to improve the performance of the model. This technique has been widely used in various computer vision tasks, including image classification, object detection, and semantic segmentation. The idea behind context-aware feature fusion is to leverage the strengths of different levels of features to improve the overall performance of the model. For example, low-level features may capture fine-grained details, while high-level features may capture more abstract information. By fusing these features, the model can benefit from both levels of information. In recent years, several context-aware feature fusion methods have been proposed, including feature pyramid networks \cite{arxivID-1612.03144-13}, attention-based feature fusion \cite{arxivID-2106.07550-14}, and non-local feature fusion \cite{arxivID-1711.07971-15}

