\subsection{Prediction and Planning}
The prediction and planning modules are two critical components of the autonomous driving system. The prediction module takes the past observations of surrounding agents and outputs their future trajectories. The planning module takes the predicted trajectories of surrounding agents as input and outputs a smooth and safe trajectory for the autonomous vehicle. The prediction module is often decoupled from the planning module, which leads to a sub-optimal prediction that does not take the planning module into account. The planning module is usually designed with a specific cost function that is hard to specify and tune, especially for the non-differentiable components like the collision avoidance term. To tackle these issues, we propose an end-to-end differentiable framework that integrates the prediction and planning modules and is able to learn the cost function from data. The proposed framework is trained on a large-scale real-world driving dataset to imitate human driving trajectories in the entire driving scene and validated in both open-loop and closed-loop manners. The open-loop testing results reveal that the proposed method outperforms the baseline methods across a variety of metrics and delivers planning-centric prediction results, allowing the planning module to output close-to-human trajectories. In closed-loop testing, the proposed method shows the ability to handle complex urban driving scenarios and robustness against the distributional shift that imitation learning methods suffer from. Importantly, we find that joint training of planning and prediction modules achieves better performance than planning with a separate trained prediction module in both open-loop and closed-loop tests. Moreover, the ablation study indicates that the learnable components in the framework are essential to ensure planning stability and performance. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first work that jointly trains the prediction and planning modules in an end-to-end differentiable framework.

\subsection{Differentiable Planning}
Differentiable planning methods have been proposed to solve the planning problem with deep neural networks. These methods typically use a differentiable nonlinear optimizer as the planner, which takes the predicted trajectories of surrounding agents given by the neural network as input and optimizes the trajectory for the autonomous vehicle. The differentiable nonlinear optimizer can be used as a layer in the neural network, which allows the entire planning system to be trained end-to-end. This approach has been shown to be effective in various planning tasks, including autonomous driving \cite{arxivID-2207.10422-0}, robotic manipulation \cite{arxivID-2208.12250-1}, and motion planning \cite{arxivID-1907.09591-2}. The differentiable planning framework has also been extended to other tasks, such as model predictive control \cite{arxivID-1810.13400-3}, multi-agent planning \cite{arxivID-2104.09705-4}, and reinforcement learning \cite{arxivID-2209.12295-5}. These methods have shown promising results in various applications and have the potential to revolutionize the way we approach planning problems. Our work extends this approach to the task of autonomous driving, where we integrate prediction and planning modules into a single differentiable framework that is trained end-to-end. This allows us to optimize the entire system jointly and improve the performance of both modules.

