 Networks are ubiquitous in nature, and are important in numerous scientific disciplines including social science, computer science and biology. These networks arise from interactions between entities and can be represented as a graph, where vertices (nodes) model entities and edges model relationships between these entities. Graph metrics are used to analyse,  classify and compare networks. Graph metrics are typically used to quantify structural properties of networks. For example, degree centrality is used to quantify the number of neighbours a vertex has. The rich-club, a generalisation of degree centrality, is used to quantify the extent to which high degree vertices are connected to each other. In this paper, we introduce a new concept called vertex participation, which is a generalisation of the rich-club, and we show how it can be used to identify a rich-club within a rich-club. We then introduce a new concept called edge participation, which is similar to vertex participation except that the focus is on edges instead of vertices. We present experimental results on both synthetic and real-world networks. We also demonstrate both concepts on a complex brain network and compare our results to the rich-club of the brain.

The rich-club is a generalisation of degree centrality that is used to quantify the extent to which high degree vertices are connected to each other \cite{arxivID-1705.02201-0}. The rich-club can be calculated as follows. Let $G = (V, E)$ be a graph, where $V$ is the set of vertices and $E$ is the set of edges. For any subset of vertices $C \subseteq V$, let $G[C]$ be the subgraph of $G$ induced by $C$. The rich-club coefficient $\phi$ is defined as follows:
\begin{equation}
\phi(k) = \frac{2|E|}{|C|(|C|-1)}
